=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The reported IT-to-CS outage does not exist in the live lab. A full diagnostic of the implicated path was performed: router0, which serves as the Layer 3 boundary between the IT segment (192.168.1.0/24, eth0) and the CS segment (192.168.2.0/24, eth1), was inspected first. Both interfaces are administratively and operationally UP with correct addresses. IP forwarding is enabled (ip_forward=1). The kernel routing table has directly connected routes for both subnets. FRR/RIP is configured to advertise both networks. Iptables has no filtering rules — all chains are ACCEPT with zero entries.

Bidirectional ping tests from it_hod (192.168.1.2) to every CS host (192.168.2.1 through 192.168.2.6) returned 0% packet loss with expected TTL of 63, confirming traffic is traversing router0. The reverse path (cs_hod → it_hod) is equally clean. Router0's interface counters show zero errors, zero drops, and zero carrier faults on both eth0 and eth1. ARP neighbors for all CS hosts are present and REACHABLE in router0's neighbor table.

Root cause: no fault exists — the IT and CS departments are fully connected through router0 as designed. The helpdesk ticket describing a cut-off between the departments does not reflect the current live state of the network.

Corrective fix: No configuration change is required. Advise the ticket submitter to re-test connectivity; if intermittent loss was observed earlier, check for transient upstream events or misidentified destination addresses at the time of the original report.
=== END ===